What's adapted vs. dropped
--------------------------

Kept from musl:
- The algorithms themselves — the NUL-sentinel byte loop (strlen,
  strcmp, strcpy, strchr), the count-driven memory loop (memcpy,
  memset, memmove), strcmp's single-subtraction three-way result,
  and memmove's copy-direction check.
- The function signatures and ASCII/byte semantics.

Dropped:
- musl's performance machinery: word-at-a-time copies, the
  has-a-zero-byte SWAR test in strlen/strchr, alignment handling,
  and the __memcpy_fwd/bwd fast paths.  The naive byte loop is the
  teaching version.
- strstr / memmem (Two-Way string matching, ~150 lines) — deferred
  to a follow-up (see tasks/libstr.md).
- strdup / strndup (need malloc) and the wide-char wcs*/wmem*
  counterparts (locale) — out of scope for this teaching libc.
